course: dictionary
lesson: 9706
title: Sulky: a face that says it all
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A sulky person is bad-tempered and quiet because they are upset or annoyed, and they want other people to notice. Look at the clues around the word. «She folded her arms, pouted and would not say a word.» Those actions show she is sulky, even though the word is not in the sentence.
explain: Sulky is an adjective, so it describes a noun: «a sulky face», «a sulky teenager». The verb is sulk, and «go off in a sulk» uses sulk as a noun. Sulkier and sulkiest show how strong the feeling is.
explain: Stories often show sulkiness through actions: slamming doors, pouting, refusing to talk. When you read these, ask yourself what the character feels. Then swap in your meaning and check that the sentence still makes sense.
explain: Words from across Britain can carry the same feeling. «You're both mardy and both guilty,» said Mum. In the Midlands and the North, «mardy» means grumpy or sulky, so Mum is saying you are both sulking. Tone and face help you read it.
fact: Sulky means bad-tempered and silent because you are upset or annoyed, and you want others to notice.
fact: Sulky is an adjective; the verb is «sulk» and the adverb is «sulkily».
fact: A sulky is also a light two-wheeled cart for one person, pulled by a horse in harness racing.
fact: «Mardy» means sulky in parts of the Midlands and the North of England.
fact: Instead of sulking, you can name your feeling and talk about it with someone you trust.
exercise: «She folded her arms, pouted and would not speak to anyone.» What is she showing? | She is delighted and excited | She is fast asleep | *She is sulky and wants others to notice | She is cold and wants a coat
exercise: «He slammed the door and went off in a sulk.» What does sulk mean here? | A new pair of shoes | *A fit of bad temper and silence | A loud song | A bag for school
exercise: Which word from the Midlands and the North means sulky? | *mardy | pet | duck | howay
test: Which word is the verb related to sulky? | sulky cart | *sulk | sulkily | sulkiest
test: «A sulky pulled by one horse» uses which meaning of sulky? | A horse race in the rain | Bad-tempered and silent | *A light two-wheeled cart | Quietly happy
exam: Jayden asks, «Can one word have two different meanings?» Which answer is right about sulky? | *Yes, sulky can mean bad-tempered or a light two-wheeled cart | No, a word has only one meaning, always | Yes, but only in Scotland | No, sulky is only ever a verb
